# rules: python-weak-random
# languages: python
# fixture: python/CWE-338.py
# from: CWE-338 (Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG), CWE-330
#
# `random` is a Mersenne Twister seeded from the clock. It is documented as
# unsuitable for security and it means it: 624 consecutive outputs are enough
# to recover the state and predict every draw after them. `secrets` is the
# same amount of code and is the fix.
#
# What it is not is a reason to report every draw in the program. A shuffle, a
# retry jitter, a random colour and a sample of test data are all `random` and
# all correct, and a rule that fires on them teaches people to switch the rule
# off - which is worse than never having written it. This is the same shape as
# swift-insecure-random-for-secret and it reads the same evidence.
#
# The name the result is given is that evidence. A programmer who calls it
# `token`, `nonce` or `otp` has said what it is for, and the syntax says
# nothing else about which kind of draw this is. A draw passed straight to
# something rather than bound to a name is not reported, which is a real gap
# and the quiet direction to miss in.

["$NAME = $DRAW"] as $binds
| "(?i)token|nonce|salt|^iv$|secret|key$|passw|otp|challenge|session"
  + "|verifier|csrf|seed|_id$|code$" as $namesASecret
| "\\brandom\\.(?:random|randint|choice|choices|uniform|randrange|shuffle"
  + "|sample|randbytes|getrandbits)\\s*\\(" as $guessableDraw
| scan_ast("*.py"; $binds) as $all
| ( $all | of($binds)
    | where_capture("NAME"; $namesASecret)
    | where_capture("DRAW"; $guessableDraw)
    | focus("DRAW") )
| finding("python-weak-random";
    "this is a Mersenne Twister seeded from the clock, and the name says the "
    + "value is meant to be unguessable; draw it from `secrets` instead")
| report
